I left out the `last-input` recording. The node's `with` clause grants only `http-client`, `notifier`, and `wall-clock`, and there is no state-store interface among them. Importing a state handle would widen the node's authority beyond what it was given, so I did not add one. The output is unchanged by this omission.

Two other decisions: the fetch uses `fetch` rather than `get`, so a transport failure becomes `fetched = 0` instead of a trap. If `notify` returns `false`, the body panics, so the node never emits a report that violates `ensures notified == true`.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{heartbeat-report};

    import aap:caps/http-client@0.1.0;
    import aap:caps/notifier@0.1.0;
    import wasi:clocks/wall-clock@0.2.0;

    export run: func(feed-url: string) -> heartbeat-report;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::http_client;
use crate::aap::caps::notifier;
use crate::wasi::clocks::wall_clock;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(feed_url: String) -> HeartbeatReport {
        let seconds = wall_clock::now().seconds;

        let fetched = match http_client::fetch(&feed_url) {
            Ok(body) => u32::try_from(body.chars().count()).unwrap_or(u32::MAX),
            Err(_) => 0,
        };

        let message = format!("heartbeat seconds={seconds} fetched={fetched}");
        let notified = notifier::notify(&message);

        if !notified {
            // Refuse to emit a report that would violate `ensures notified == true`.
            panic!("notifier refused the heartbeat message");
        }

        HeartbeatReport {
            seconds,
            fetched,
            notified,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

